  DEFENDING ARMY

  Kahn marched with his loyal troops, jaws set in grim resolution.  No 
generals were present to lead the forces of Uga-Buga Makumb.
  Kahn was unlucky this day, for a number of his skirmishers got lost in a 
swamp.  The acting commander thus lucked into a significant edge.  As the 
desperate battle developed, the tactical wit of the acting commander and 
Kahn seemed to be evenly matched and neither leader could achieve a tactical 
advantage.

  On the right outside flank, the 218th Legion were arrayed against the 
121st Garrison.  The 220th Light Cavalry outflanked the undermanned enemy 
and charged in with full force.  On this field, some soldiers of the 121st 
Garrison noticed the presence of an encampment of trollops behind their 
opponents' lines, so they valiantly concentrated their efforts to smash 
through to victory.
  But then the tide of battle reversed.  The army general appeared to 
personally lead the 218th Legion in the front lines, rallying them to a 
truly excellent effort.  The 218th Legion battled with the 121st Garrison 
and managed to make some measure of progress.
  Out on the left outside wing, the 188th Bowmen were blessed by a retinue 
of medicine men sent by the League of Pagan Clerics, which aroused them to a 
fanatical zeal.  They faced the 153rd Garrison.  In this match, the 188th 
Bowmen carelessly blundered into a nest of booby traps and lost some 
irreplaceable front-line troops.  The 188th Bowmen battled with the 153rd 
Garrison and fought courageously, but was steadily pushed back.
  The acting commander's armies vanquished the army opposing them with a 
final charge.
  Though the provincial garrison commander's host were victorious, Kahn 
regrouped his forces and prepared to fight for Bronzeleaf.
  The wet spring blossomed into a fragrant early summer.  Uga-Buga Makumb 
worked hard on his Necromancy but was thwarted in his efforts.

  In Bronzeleaf, Kahn brought to bay Shaman Uga-Buga Makumb Stuffy doll.  
Kahn commanded the attacking armies.  The defending forces were commanded by 
the provincial garrison commander.  The armies were drawn up into lines of 
battle as shown here:

  ATTACKING ARMY, Battle of Bronzeleaf (1303):

