  The attackers had the 321st Eagles, the 322nd Eagles and the 326th Dragons 
in reserve.  The defenders had the 330th Chariots in reserve.  I commanded a 
host of loyal soldiers.  Ningarl stood ready at the head of his thousands.
  I stepped forward and challenged Ningarl to honorable combat.  Ningarl 
answered the challenge to honorable combat.  I chanted my strange words of 
magic and cast a Spiritwrack spell, to make Ningarl quake and shiver with 
spiritual convulsions.
  Ningarl speedily moved into bow range.  I chanted my strange words of 
magic and cast a Holy Symbol spell.  A golden mist in the shape of a cross 
formed around me.
  Ningarl closed to melee range, seething with blood-lust.  Showing no fear, 
Ningarl moved closer and swang at me but his aim was just off the mark.  
Teleko's spell was disrupted by the event.  I swang at Ningarl and walloped 
him with 6 damage points.  Ningarl thrust at me and scored 1 damage points 
on me.
  I deftly swang at Ningarl and damaged him, inflicting 5 damage points.  
Ningarl fell, fatally injured.  Thus did Ningarl die, a defiant death by any 
standard.
  The forces commanded by Ningarl were disheartened by his demise, yet still 
they moved forward with some hope of victory.  Teleko's luck wasn't very 
good this time.  My superstitious men became unsettled when my priests were 
unable to properly sacrifice a goat to the gods because they had all been 
stolen by gryphons.  On the other hand tactical superiority was clearly 
guaranteed by my powerful tactical genius.
  The Dwarves commanded by Ningarl were not impressed by his lack of 
leadership ability.
  Across the entire front, the Veteran 323rd Trolls were arrayed against the 
216th Axemen, who sang out their battle hymns, inspired with a spirit of 
holy, fanatical fury by their exalted King.  The 323rd Trolls met their 
match against the doughty Axemen, tragically unable to avoid having their 
long legs cut to shreds.  The 320th Eagles soared over the 48th Chariots and 
attacked them.  The 319th Eagles swooped down on the 227th Light Cavalry and 
attacked them.  The 321st Eagles swooped down on the 330th Chariots and 
attacked without mercy.  The 322nd Eagles soared over the 216th Axemen and 
attacked them.
  I maneuvered the 320th Eagles to the fore, as a strategem for a superior 
nemesis effect.  The 320th Eagles fought with the 216th Axemen and cut them 
down like beanstalks.
  The forces of Teleko broke through the lines of their opposition and were 
victorious.  The 320th Eagles slew the 216th Axemen almost to a man.  The 
323rd Trolls scattered the 48th Chariots in all directions.  The 319th 
Eagles destroyed the 227th Light Cavalry.  The 321st Eagles annihilated the 
330th Chariots.
  Cretacia was overrun by Teleko's men in a hard-fought campaign.  The 
battle affected the quality of our forces:  we could not have been more 
pleased with the fighting spirit of the 322nd Eagles, and we have raised its 
rating to Crack.  Its forces completely destroyed, the acting commander fled 
alone to Cherrywood.
  In early winter, I rode my flying armies over to Cherrywood.  West of here 
is Amberleaf (#401), a Forest area owned by Hokuto, where Elves dwell.  
Their Elven warriors array themselves in the style of Axemen.  To the north 
icy wastes stretch to meet the far horizon.

  An army sent by the empire of Shaman Uga-Buga Makumb Stuffy doll entered 
Cherrywood, hoping to subjugate that province owned by Hokuto (#4) and made 
up of great Forests.  Cherrywood was conquered and occupied by Teleko's 
host.  Cherrywood was found to have an income of 3 gold.

  Thy humble servant,   Teleko


