  DEFENDING ARMY

  The attackers had the 249th Dragons, the 198th Trolls, the 102nd Eagles 
and the 123rd Eagles in reserve.  I stood ready at the head of my thousands. 
York commanded a host of loyal soldiers.
  The luck of Odysseus's men was running high for this momentous occasion, 
for my men were in good spirits from a recent holiday feast, and I thus had 
a considerable edge.  As the fierce battle developed, the tactical abilities 
of York and I appeared to be well matched and neither general could gain a 
tactical advantage.

  Across the whole front, the Veteran 333rd Bowmen were arrayed against the 
118th Legion.  The 334th Bowmen flanked the enemy lines and attacked with 
full strength.  The 206th Dragons swooped down on the 118th Legion and 
attacked them.  The 198th Trolls outflanked the enemy and attacked with full 
force.  The 333rd Bowmen battled the 118th Legion and mowed them down like 
weeds.
  Having found a vulnerable point in the line of the host of York, a final 
determined slashing attack finished off their enemy and won the battle for 
me.  The 333rd Bowmen scattered the 118th Legion in all directions.
  Ravenwood was overrun by Odysseus's men in a bloody campaign.  The battle 
affected the quality of our forces:  the 334th Bowmen fought determinedly 
during the battle and came through with a good effort.  We can now rank it a 
Crack unit.  Odysseus's experience improved my skill in Tactics.
  With the advent of the fall season, I rode my flying armies into Dirkwood. 
To the north lay an icy wasteland which extends as far as a man can see.

  A host of savage warriors carried the banner of 
Shaman Uga-Buga Makumb Stuffy doll into the Jungle province of Dirkwood, 
held by the provincial Dragon chieftain.  I commanded the attacking armies.  
The defenders were led by the provincial Dragon chieftain.  The armies were 
drawn up into lines of battle as shown here:

  ATTACKING ARMY, Battle of Dirkwood (1201):

